{
  "sampleSet": "living-folio-overview-v1",
  "entries": [
    {
      "sampleKey": "notebook",
      "sampleSet": "living-folio-overview-v1",
      "schemaVersion": 2,
      "type": "Essay",
      "title": "The notebook as a way of thinking",
      "description": "On unfinished thoughts, attention, and why a notebook should be more than storage.",
      "originalDate": "2026-11-06",
      "body": {
        "type": "doc",
        "attrs": {
          "annotations": {
            "source": {
              "id": "source",
              "kind": "footnote",
              "text": "Demonstration source note: this sample essay was written for the prototype. Replace it with your own words and sources when preparing real writing.",
              "label": "In the margin",
              "showLabel": true
            },
            "question": {
              "id": "question",
              "kind": "footnote",
              "text": "The companion note develops the woodpecker question as a small exercise in looking closely.",
              "label": "In the margin",
              "showLabel": true
            },
            "workbench": {
              "id": "workbench",
              "kind": "side",
              "text": "Leave an idea visible for a day. It may ask a better question the second time you pass it.",
              "label": "A small experiment",
              "showLabel": true
            },
            "loose": {
              "id": "loose",
              "kind": "side",
              "text": "Connections can be useful before they become explanations.",
              "label": "A loose thread",
              "showLabel": true
            }
          }
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "attrs": {
              "blockId": "sampled3952813302b4441894b912ade5dca19",
              "annotations": []
            },
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Illustrative sample writing for exploring this website.",
                "marks": [
                  {
                    "type": "italic"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "attrs": {
              "blockId": "sampled679e4a2ac2244dc8102ce1e3c87b167",
              "annotations": []
            },
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "For a long time, I thought a notebook was somewhere to keep an idea safe. A sentence arrived; I wrote it down. A useful observation appeared; I gave it a page. The book filled slowly, and I felt that something had been accomplished."
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "attrs": {
              "blockId": "samplebfaa93044f3f4e6fb217a89e8fdb5977",
              "annotations": []
            },
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "But keeping a thought is different from working on it. When I returned to those pages, many of the sentences seemed complete only because I had stopped asking questions. The notebook had preserved the first version of an idea, including all the things I had failed to notice."
              },
              {
                "type": "footnote",
                "attrs": {
                  "id": "source"
                }
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "heading",
            "attrs": {
              "blockId": "samplef2138a9ec2de4bc98a9581b952d0dbf9",
              "annotations": [],
              "level": 2
            },
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Give a thought somewhere to go"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "attrs": {
              "blockId": "samplef838ce9d48244342a5b15ab60cc4f6a1",
              "annotations": []
            },
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "I am beginning to think of a notebook as "
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "a workbench rather than a cupboard",
                "marks": [
                  {
                    "type": "annotation",
                    "attrs": {
                      "id": "workbench"
                    }
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": ". A place to put something unfinished where I can turn it around. A drawing can interrupt an argument. A question can remain a question for a while. Two unrelated observations can share a page without being forced into a conclusion."
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "attrs": {
              "blockId": "sampled03349d9908247fb8b08a7a952de5a87",
              "annotations": []
            },
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "The important part is the return. A page becomes interesting when the person reading it is no longer quite the person who wrote it. A week is sometimes enough. A conversation, a failed attempt, or an afternoon making something with my hands can change the meaning of an old sentence."
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "blockquote",
            "attrs": {
              "blockId": "sampled762b251a56b47ec866910b2a67f5350",
              "annotations": []
            },
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "attrs": {
                  "blockId": "sample6f3c241d984e4a3cbe3d888578f88fff",
                  "annotations": []
                },
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "A good page does not only remember what I thought. It gives me a reason to think again."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "heading",
            "attrs": {
              "blockId": "sampleb6c107d0d4af4b80b3fcf75d2704c52e",
              "annotations": [],
              "level": 2
            },
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Keep the edges open"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "attrs": {
              "blockId": "samplea9be01f8acff4513b71006ed19e840ed",
              "annotations": []
            },
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "This changes the kind of things worth writing down. Not only conclusions, but the small instructions that might lead somewhere: look at the underside; draw the joint again; find out how the woodpecker’s tongue works."
              },
              {
                "type": "footnote",
                "attrs": {
                  "id": "question"
                }
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "attrs": {
              "blockId": "samplec4a30deeb68f4a0ba6768594f3dc7104",
              "annotations": [
                "loose"
              ]
            },
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "There is room here for an essay, a diagram, an unfinished object, and a note that turns out to be wrong. They do not need to agree yet. Their closeness may be the useful thing."
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "attrs": {
              "blockId": "sample7d4eaa19a29842a0bb2ec1d2892589c5",
              "annotations": []
            },
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "What I want from this notebook is not an archive that proves I have been thinking. I want a practice that changes what I notice when I leave the page."
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      "paragraphs": [],
      "notes": [],
      "connected": [
        "woodpecker",
        "unfinished",
        "shapes"
      ],
      "published": null,
      "dirty": false
    },
    {
      "sampleKey": "woodpecker",
      "sampleSet": "living-folio-overview-v1",
      "schemaVersion": 2,
      "type": "Note",
      "title": "Describe the tongue of the woodpecker",
      "description": "A small question, carried around for a few days. A reminder to look more closely.",
      "originalDate": "2026-11-05",
      "body": {
        "type": "doc",
        "attrs": {
          "annotations": {
            "practice": {
              "id": "practice",
              "kind": "side",
              "text": "This note connects back to the essay about notebooks and forward to the sketch about two shapes.",
              "label": "Try this",
              "showLabel": true
            }
          }
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "attrs": {
              "blockId": "sample58f682c8128f4c64941a1872bdaae84d",
              "annotations": []
            },
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Illustrative sample writing for exploring this website.",
                "marks": [
                  {
                    "type": "italic"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "attrs": {
              "blockId": "samplecd1f59ece5e14701b4dad46f37c72341",
              "annotations": []
            },
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "I like a question that is small enough to carry in a pocket. Not “How does nature work?” but “What happens when this particular bird reaches into a tree?”"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "attrs": {
              "blockId": "sample79e4e6c96e6c43f1aa39123cf29cd047",
              "annotations": []
            },
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "The smaller question asks something more useful of me. It asks me to look, sketch, read, and admit what I cannot yet explain. It gives attention a direction without promising an answer."
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "attrs": {
              "blockId": "samplebb9ff6d9649a40eab47dd84d4ec801eb",
              "annotations": [
                "practice"
              ]
            },
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Today’s version of the exercise: choose one ordinary thing and describe it without reaching for a general lesson."
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "bulletList",
            "attrs": {
              "blockId": "sample59d06f586ae643469a0449bd8aea50a9",
              "annotations": []
            },
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "listItem",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "paragraph",
                    "attrs": {
                      "blockId": "samplef9b19be831f24541acde735917bf1ce3",
                      "annotations": []
                    },
                    "content": [
                      {
                        "type": "text",
                        "text": "Start with what can be seen."
                      }
                    ]
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "listItem",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "paragraph",
                    "attrs": {
                      "blockId": "sample6d76b18b4a204d59aa35854119e0bbd0",
                      "annotations": []
                    },
                    "content": [
                      {
                        "type": "text",
                        "text": "Draw the part that is difficult to describe."
                      }
                    ]
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "listItem",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "paragraph",
                    "attrs": {
                      "blockId": "sample5f165b36cdbe4c6886cc37f1b726a0a2",
                      "annotations": []
                    },
                    "content": [
                      {
                        "type": "text",
                        "text": "Write down the next question, not a conclusion."
                      }
                    ]
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "attrs": {
              "blockId": "sample60ff3fc031af4a358aa2bbb33405619c",
              "annotations": []
            },
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "A handle. A hinge. The way a leaf joins its stem. There are enough questions within reach for a lifetime."
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      "paragraphs": [],
      "notes": [],
      "connected": [
        "notebook",
        "shapes"
      ],
      "published": null,
      "dirty": false
    },
    {
      "sampleKey": "unfinished",
      "sampleSet": "living-folio-overview-v1",
      "schemaVersion": 2,
      "type": "Note",
      "title": "A place for the unfinished",
      "description": "Some things need to stay on the workbench a little longer.",
      "originalDate": "2026-11-03",
      "body": {
        "type": "doc",
        "attrs": {
          "annotations": {
            "waiting": {
              "id": "waiting",
              "kind": "side",
              "text": "Unfinished is not the same as abandoned. The difference may be attention.",
              "label": "Still taking shape",
              "showLabel": true
            }
          }
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "attrs": {
              "blockId": "samplea2976500c2774d5bbddc1d0e4c799a6b",
              "annotations": []
            },
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Illustrative sample writing for exploring this website.",
                "marks": [
                  {
                    "type": "italic"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "attrs": {
              "blockId": "sample16e90bd8f3d04e0da3a46fece2e49e98",
              "annotations": []
            },
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "There is a particular relief in putting a half-made thing down without putting it away. The next step can remain a little uncertain while the work stays visible."
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "attrs": {
              "blockId": "sample666c3df4a21742229b97a1d62253e40d",
              "annotations": [
                "waiting"
              ]
            },
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "I used to read that uncertainty as a failure of discipline. Sometimes it is. But sometimes the unfinished part is telling me where the interesting question begins."
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "attrs": {
              "blockId": "sample5192ee13c5ee4e6a96ac8e52f69d7f45",
              "annotations": []
            },
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "The distinction may be whether I return. An unfinished thing that I keep meeting can become a conversation. An unfinished thing that I carefully hide becomes an excuse."
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "attrs": {
              "blockId": "sample62a672f3d2bc46b1ad7075c9b795e50f",
              "annotations": []
            },
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "For now, I am leaving a small space on the desk for work that has not decided what it wants to be."
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      "paragraphs": [],
      "notes": [],
      "connected": [
        "notebook",
        "slowly"
      ],
      "published": null,
      "dirty": false
    },
    {
      "sampleKey": "slowly",
      "sampleSet": "living-folio-overview-v1",
      "schemaVersion": 2,
      "type": "Essay",
      "title": "On doing one thing slowly",
      "description": "A study in repetition, feedback, and the useful limits of a small task.",
      "originalDate": "2026-11-02",
      "body": {
        "type": "doc",
        "attrs": {
          "annotations": {
            "feedback": {
              "id": "feedback",
              "kind": "side",
              "text": "A useful note records the conditions too: what changed, what stayed the same, and what is still uncertain.",
              "label": "On the bench",
              "showLabel": true
            }
          }
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "attrs": {
              "blockId": "sample4bfbbaefaf724102a622430ed6c27974",
              "annotations": []
            },
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Illustrative sample writing for exploring this website.",
                "marks": [
                  {
                    "type": "italic"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "attrs": {
              "blockId": "samplecccdf0c2a4214599b2794ad4c60afa85",
              "annotations": []
            },
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "A small task has an advantage over a grand intention: it can answer back. Draw the same curve ten times and the tenth curve knows something the first did not. The paper makes the difference visible."
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "attrs": {
              "blockId": "samplea740993169184c1c8cb98782cd6a1546",
              "annotations": []
            },
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Speed can hide this conversation. I finish the gesture before I have noticed which part of it needs attention. Slowing down does not make the work meaningful by itself, but it lets the feedback arrive."
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "heading",
            "attrs": {
              "blockId": "sample880cf811a3a84bd4a23f1b320592227a",
              "annotations": [],
              "level": 2
            },
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "A modest practice"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "orderedList",
            "attrs": {
              "blockId": "sample153e8e75179042178ea9c95bd612b153",
              "annotations": [],
              "start": 1
            },
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "listItem",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "paragraph",
                    "attrs": {
                      "blockId": "sample17bb5a554748478da9bb373c550854e8",
                      "annotations": []
                    },
                    "content": [
                      {
                        "type": "text",
                        "text": "Choose one gesture that takes less than a minute."
                      }
                    ]
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "listItem",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "paragraph",
                    "attrs": {
                      "blockId": "sample83c99fee5ec04645a5544f1576c1ec97",
                      "annotations": []
                    },
                    "content": [
                      {
                        "type": "text",
                        "text": "Repeat it until a difference becomes visible."
                      }
                    ]
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "type": "listItem",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "paragraph",
                    "attrs": {
                      "blockId": "sampledf9ca2c913ae40dc9c27eaba2d0a5656",
                      "annotations": []
                    },
                    "content": [
                      {
                        "type": "text",
                        "text": "Change one thing, and notice what follows."
                      }
                    ]
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "attrs": {
              "blockId": "sample0b36f16b99a2438db02ac84514af65fd",
              "annotations": [
                "feedback"
              ]
            },
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "The point is not to perform patience. The point is to make a change small enough to understand. A line that wavers, a tool that catches, a sentence that refuses to settle: each offers a particular kind of information."
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "blockquote",
            "attrs": {
              "blockId": "sample1961158cd6fd4b32954b1bc6d721c1fb",
              "annotations": []
            },
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "attrs": {
                  "blockId": "sample3e3e3ec0d27147098a40e6f545e3418c",
                  "annotations": []
                },
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Work at a speed where you can still hear what the material is telling you."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "attrs": {
              "blockId": "sample7762094aa5454c2d85856d794de7420d",
              "annotations": []
            },
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "This is another reason to keep a notebook nearby. The page can catch the observation before I turn it into a rule. “This worked today” is often more honest than “This is how it works.”"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "attrs": {
              "blockId": "sample5d690eeb71c942cd8a9b29cbb21a0d49",
              "annotations": []
            },
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "A finished object may be the result. A more careful way of noticing may be the thing I keep."
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      "paragraphs": [],
      "notes": [],
      "connected": [
        "unfinished",
        "notebook"
      ],
      "published": null,
      "dirty": false
